{:edb/db-before-t 1784 :edb/db-after-t 1785 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535097 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:44:48.753Z" 13194139535097 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331032 13194139535097 true] [17592186184128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331046 13194139535097 true] [17592186184129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331025 13194139535097 true] [17592186184129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186331039 13194139535097 true] [17592186184740 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535097 true] [17592186184740 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x =-2$ is given by $P_{2}(x) = 2 + 4(x + 2) + 16(x + 2)^{2}$. What is the value of $f^{″}(-2)$?" 13194139535097 false] [17592186184740 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=2+4(x+2)+16(x+2)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-2)?$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186184740 1145 "This is an expansion about the point $x=-2,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-2).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $16.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-2)=16\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-2)=32.\n$$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331026 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1089 "q-15519" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/assets/b70f90738fc329a0d58527dc013a3e175b0954e0ccc27972d5abcde1708e1bca.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=2?$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1113 #uuid "d2528da7-7989-5718-98f8-9e34c6fc3006" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331025 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is zero. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be zero.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1+(x-2)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331027 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331028 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331029 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331030 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331031 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1041 "selection" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331027 13194139535097 true] [17592186331026 1110 #uuid "821ac992-a5a1-5ec2-a880-570fb0418e8c" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331027 1034 "1+(x-2)^{2}" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331027 1111 #uuid "25260566-424c-56b3-99bd-90a44452693e" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331028 1034 "1-{x}^{2}" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331028 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331028 1111 #uuid "0517db9f-bbdc-5dcf-b086-db21f6afaebd" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331029 1034 "1-(x-2)^{2}" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331029 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331029 1111 #uuid "48cb5ae2-0240-50fc-b00a-4e128e10719c" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331030 1034 "2+(x-2)-{(x-2)}^{2}" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331030 1111 #uuid "d718e0a5-a0c5-5bcf-aa94-a92aa16e8a6a" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331031 1034 "1+(x-2)-{(x-2)}^{2}" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331031 1111 #uuid "deaaf7b2-ca28-50e3-b9be-275aa28e9d51" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331033 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1089 "q-81062" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1100 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-24+36(x+3)-18(x+3)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-3)?$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1113 #uuid "2c7eb72b-51e7-5594-a379-c3d96c8cc9aa" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331032 1145 "This is an expansion about the point $x=-3,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-18.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3)=-18\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-3)=-36.\n$$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331034 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331035 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331036 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331037 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331038 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1041 "selection" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331034 13194139535097 true] [17592186331033 1110 #uuid "9df7f574-f220-5cdd-b5b3-e23a8a279b59" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331034 1034 "-36" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331034 1111 #uuid "65efb53e-5e59-573e-bbc8-66fc305cd7af" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331035 1034 "-18" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331035 1111 #uuid "b12e1f62-4c7b-5147-b017-f88da94fb738" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331036 1034 "36" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331036 1111 #uuid "18dfa965-fe8a-5b38-9d99-98a1c590e4d8" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331037 1034 "-72" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331037 1111 #uuid "f18a85a0-6d21-518c-bbb6-6d0e3ede348d" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331038 1034 "48" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535097 true] [17592186331038 1111 #uuid "675ef4a9-b374-5104-ade8-4fc9ebda7aa4" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331040 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1089 "q-81064" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053574/assets/185b5924c542bb9f396fe826aac141256a13b967c45c47afc87ca07445df062f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-1?$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1113 #uuid "676584ae-8337-52da-aa8f-4d12ff4111d0" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331039 1145 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-1)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535097 true] [17592186331040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331041 13194139535097 true] [175921